{"availability":"available","full":"huemorgan2/say-hi","name":"say-hi","description":"Can a model that only picks from a list learn to talk? JEV types every reply one key at a time.","category":"Education","language":"JavaScript","stars":2,"forks":2,"created":"2026-09-30T18:04:35Z","updated":"2026-09-30T18:48:31Z","license":"AGPL-3.0","branch":"main","homepage":null,"topics":["chatbot","experiment","jev","typesafe"],"demo":null,"color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T10:45:07.783Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-03T10:45:07.783Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-03T10:45:07.783Z","lastDemoCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T10:45:07.783Z","discoveredVia":[],"metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-03T10:45:07.783Z","stars":2,"forks":2}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Can a model that only picks from a list learn to talk?","JEV (TypeSafe's jev-1.13.0) isn't a text generator. It's a chooser: you send it a situation (state) and a question with a set of options (criteria), and it picks one, with a confidence and a probability for every option. say-hi tries to get it to talk anyway, by giving it a keyboard. Every reply is typed one key per JEV request: letters, digits, punctuation, space, backspace, return and a SEND key.","It's a fun experiment with Jev, and a starting point for simple chatbots with canned responses: replace the keyboard (or the answer list) with your own canned replies, and Jev picks the best one for each message."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/huemorgan2/say-hi/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"4c662872cff9c8e94637c01a65be8d6195607eb1f784a60e5a6bf732f251ff33","extractedAt":"2026-10-03T10:45:07.783Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T10:45:07.783Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Can a model that only picks from a list learn to talk?","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/huemorgan2/say-hi/blob/main/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[],"fingerprint":"4c662872cff9c8e94637c01a65be8d6195607eb